{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786429317,"nonce":"k9ZmtZ3G1TemDO2YgAKqGtRgWqE=","title":"runbook.link: four defects that put a wrong or missing link on the card","message":"**What is wrong.** Four defects in `internal/enrichment/enrichers/runbook/`, all found while writing its first tests and all pinned as skipped `TestBUG_*` rather than fixed:\n\n1. **An unresolved org-template placeholder ships as the Slack runbook button.** `expand`'s own doc says an unfilled `{label}` 'fails normalisation and is reported as rejected'. It does not: `validate.IsAbsoluteHTTPURL` accepts `{`/`}` in a path, `url.URL.String()` percent-encodes them, and `https://wiki.example/org/%7Bregion%7D/runbook` is hoisted into `Payload.Runbook`, which SPEC §F.2 renders as the card's button. **This is the failure class the enrichment issue named: not a missing field, a field that looks populated and is wrong.**\n\n2. **`normalise` uses the `alert_sources.base_url` predicate on operator links.** `IsAbsoluteHTTPURL` deliberately refuses a query string, a fragment and a trailing slash — correct for a base URL that gets API paths concatenated onto it, wrong for a runbook or dashboard link. Confirmed rejected: `https://grafana.example/d/abc?from=now-1h\u0026to=now`, `https://wiki.example/runbook#step-2`, `https://wiki.example/runbooks/`. The comment above `normalise` claims the opposite ('the fragment and the query are preserved verbatim, because a dashboard link without its time range is a link to the wrong thing'). Degradation is honest, but the card loses its runbook button for a large fraction of real alerts and blames the operator's annotation.\n\n3. **A rejected link is reported as `StatusSkipped`, not `partial`.** `Enrich` sets `partial` when anything was rejected, then unconditionally overwrites with `skipped` when the link list is empty — which is exactly the case the report exists for. `StatusSkipped` is documented as 'Applicable said no' (`Applicable` said yes), `Succeeded()` is false for it and `Reusable` is false for it, so a run that looked and found a broken annotation is filed identically to one that never ran. Both sibling enrichers use the other convention.\n\n4. **An uppercase scheme is rejected outright.** `looksLikeURL` tests `strings.HasPrefix(v, \"https://\")` case-sensitively and `validate.HTTPURLRe` is `^https?://[^\\s]+$` with no `(?i)`, so `HTTPS://wiki.example/…` never reaches the lowercasing — and `normalise`'s `u.Scheme = strings.ToLower(u.Scheme)` is a no-op twice over (unreachable for an uppercase scheme, and `net/url.Parse` already lowercases a legal one). RFC 3986 §3.1 makes schemes case-insensitive, so a good link is filed as unusable.\n\n**Why it matters.** Enrichment is rendered into the Slack card and the alert detail view, so these are wrong statements shown to a human deciding whether to act at 3am. (1) is the worst — a button that leads somewhere that does not exist.\n\n**Where.** `internal/enrichment/enrichers/runbook/runbook.go`, `internal/platform/validate/`.\n\n**Done when.** A placeholder that did not expand is rejected rather than shipped; operator links are validated with a predicate that permits queries, fragments and trailing slashes; a rejected link reports `partial`; schemes are matched case-insensitively. The four `TestBUG_*` tests unskip as each is fixed.","files":null}]}